WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.720
VOICE OVER: This is the moment that the&nbsp;
Ingenuity helicopter made history,

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.760
making the first ever powered&nbsp;
flight on another planet.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:16.000
ANUBHAV DATTA: It will tell&nbsp;us whether our 
understanding of how we fly is correct.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.920
So that's the first thing we want&nbsp;
to discover: can we fly on Mars?

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:26.560
So we think the math says we can, our&nbsp;
understanding of basic principles say we can,

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.840
with difficulty, but yes we can.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.760
Now this will be the proof.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:36.800
VOICE OVER: 290 million kilometers away,
NASA's Ingenuity helicopter

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.920
has taken the first step towards a new
type of space exploration.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:47.120
ANUBHAV: We have been walking on Mars and there's nothing&nbsp;wrong with that, you need to walk to discover,

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.049
but now we can run.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:59.840
VOICE OVER:  Before the historic flight Nature&nbsp;
spoke with Dr Anubhav Datta,

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.120
who was part of the team&nbsp;
that helped test the craft.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.320
ANUBHAV: In terms of range, reach&nbsp;
and resolution it's hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:12.400
beat an aircraft that can fly and can fly&nbsp;
at low speed in controlled hover mode.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.920
Particularly on Mars, the places that&nbsp;
we're interested in are up the slope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.800
down the crater, into the caves, on the cliffs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.064
and so those are very inaccessible terrain.

00:01:27.064 --> 00:01:31.200
Especially with no roads and rocky terrain on Mars.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.590
VOICE OVER: But flying on Mars is far from straight forward.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:45.440
ANUBHAV: Just to be able to carry a flying vehicle&nbsp;
292 million miles and deploy it, unfold it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:52.000
all in one piece, still alive and beaming - that&nbsp;
is an incredible achievement just by itself.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:00.640
We learned how to fly on Earth after eighty years&nbsp;
of experimentation, trial, error, flight tests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.840
wind tunnel tests, building special wind&nbsp;
tunnels, so the first challenge is obvious;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:13.120
that we don't have that chance, for trial and&nbsp;
error, and design, build, fly on another planet,

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.160
so we have to rely on simulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:24.880
and our very carefully scaled testing that we&nbsp;
can do on Earth and extrapolate our knowledge.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:28.240
VOICE OVER: But getting to Mars
safely&nbsp;is not the only problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:32.720
Mars has a much thinner atmosphere than&nbsp;
Earth and that means that the parameters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.520
flight relies on, like the viscosity&nbsp;
and density of air, are all different.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:44.800
ANUBHAV: The fundamental ingredient of lift, 
lift&nbsp;is what keeps aeroplanes afloat in air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:50.596
is viscosity, and there is viscosity&nbsp;
on Mars, so you can fly on Mars.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.160
How much lift you generate depends on density.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.680
VOICE OVER: The Martian atmosphere
 means that scientists like&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.360
Datta need to think about&nbsp;
design completely differently.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.320
ANUBHAV: When you try to design something on the&nbsp;
basis of viscosity with very low density&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:12.560
it's, it's a new kind of design that relies&nbsp;
on what is, what is called Reynolds number.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:16.000
VOICE OVER: Reynolds number is related to airflow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.364
but it's not especially relevant to&nbsp;
the design of large craft on Earth.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:25.760
ANUBHAV: Some people interested in insect flight zoom&nbsp;
into that number but in big aircraft we ignore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:31.840
that number, but that number becomes the&nbsp;
the driving factor for Martian design.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.680
VOICE OVER: Ingenuity's design had to contend with a unique&nbsp;
set of challenges different from those faced by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:44.160
craft on Earth, but to an aerospace engineer&nbsp;
these challenges also offered opportunities.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:48.800
ANUBHAV: If you think in a different way there&nbsp;
are many advantages of flying on Mars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:53.280
There's low density means low&nbsp;
drag, which means you don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:59.600
to shape the fuselage or the airframe you know&nbsp;
in a particular, particularly aerodynamic manner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:05.200
So you can have science instruments sticking&nbsp;
all around the aircraft, all you have to do is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:09.840
to let the rotor carry you to where you&nbsp;
want to go. That's an enormous benefit.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.760
VOICE OVER: Ingenuity's first flight; three&nbsp;
meters up into the Martian air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.640
followed by a 90 degree turn and a&nbsp;
safe descent may not sound like much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:24.560
but its significance for the future of planetary&nbsp;
exploration will be felt for years to come.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:30.880
ANUBHAV: You know sometimes people think 'oh it'll just&nbsp;
pop into the air and maybe not go anywhere'. Well,

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:35.840
remember for vertical lift and for helicopters&nbsp;
that's the most difficult mode of flight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:42.000
the rock-solid hover, and so that's what it's&nbsp;
going to try and attempt, so yes it's a short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.800
flight but it's by no means a simple flight,&nbsp;
in fact it is the most difficult flight.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:56.560
Oh, the implication is huge, so if we can fly&nbsp;
and give a platform to our planetary scientists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:04.160
an unparalleled platform to have access to all the&nbsp;
remote corners and interesting features of Mars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:09.840
that they don't have now, so as&nbsp;
i said it's like we were walking,

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:16.826
the last 25 years we have had rovers we have&nbsp;
covered a total of 44 miles, in total,

00:05:16.826 --> 00:05:23.840
of flat simple terrain. Now all of a sudden&nbsp;
we are opening up this new space.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:32.160
For a long time, you know conventionally&nbsp;
aeronautics is the tax you pay for space research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:36.640
because you can't get to space or come&nbsp;
back from space without aeronautics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:43.547
but now it can provide a, you know,&nbsp;
launching pad for new exploration in space.

